From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 22/39] Debye material: line hooks for the fused sweep

Engine_Ext_DebyeMaterial has its own pre and post voltage hooks (the poles' ADE states), which had no
line versions, so a Debye material kept the multithreaded engine on the unfused plan with these two
hooks on one thread. DoPreVoltageUpdatesLines() and DoPostVoltageUpdatesLines() run the same per-position
operations for the positions on the x-lines of the call, in x order, through the float index tables the
dispersive line hooks already build; the apply hook is the inherited line hook. Every position has its
own state and reads only its own cell (bit-identical).

Verified: scripts/checks/debye_check.sh (Debye slab, basic, sse-compressed, multithreaded 1 and 4
threads), Debye blocks with Mur and PML_8, mixed with lumped elements, at 1, 3 and 4 threads against the
template hooks of the single-threaded engine, the validation suite. Upstream's dispersive case (a Lorentz
and a Debye block, Mur, a sinusoidal source with the steady-state detection, 1 M cells, 3000 steps), now on
the fused sweep with the previous commit, against OPENEMS_FUSED=0 in the same build (paired interleaved A/B,
5 rounds, other processes on about half a core): 40.8 -> 263.9 MC/s at 4 threads (x6.5), 43.8 -> 439.9 at 8
(x10.0): the unfused plan ran the Debye hooks on one thread.

